    "result_text": "The change is complete and the test passes. Here's what I implemented as an additive, full-stack change for **\"delete a project I no longer need \u2014 keep a record that I did.\"**\n\n**Server (new files):**\n- `DeleteProject.java` \u2014 `@RequestBody` DTO carrying the project `id`.\n- `ProjectsRemove.java` \u2014 logic class backing `POST /api/projects/remove`. It looks up the project, deletes it, and records the audit line `PROJECT_DELETED id=<id>` through the injected `Audit` service (the tested side-effect channel).\n- `officefloor/rest/api/projects/remove.POST.yml` \u2014 wires the endpoint (path under `/api/` so it bypasses the SPA fallback).\n\n**Front-end (one new slot file):**\n- `features/projects/delete.slot.tsx` \u2014 fills the existing `ProjectRowActions` slot with a `data-testid=\"project-delete-<id>\"` button. It's a `useMutation` that POSTs the id, then `invalidateQueries(['projects'])` so the list refetches and the row drops out. No existing page was edited.\n\n**Notes on choices:**\n- No schema migration was needed \u2014 deletion uses the existing `project` table and adds no columns/constraints, so I didn't add a no-op Flyway migration.\n- I followed the established mutation idiom (`POST .../remove` like line-item removal) rather than an HTTP `DELETE`, since `http.ts` and the existing endpoints only use GET/POST.\n\nVerification: `npm run typecheck` passes and `bin/e2e` reports the acceptance spec passing (row removed, count drops to 1, and the `PROJECT_DELETED id=1` audit line present).",
